WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.992
In this video you'll learn the picture that janus,

2
00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:07.927
who posts as @repligate, draws of a transformer:

3
00:00:07.927 --> 00:00:10.064
one column for every token,

4
00:00:10.064 --> 00:00:13.326
one row for every layer, and the vertical

5
00:00:13.326 --> 00:00:16.112
highway called the residual stream.

6
00:00:16.112 --> 00:00:18.527
A transformer reading text is a grid,

7
00:00:18.527 --> 00:00:20.059
a copy of the same network

8
00:00:20.059 --> 00:00:23.206
at every token position, stacked in layers;

9
00:00:23.206 --> 00:00:25.563
the residual stream carries each position's

10
00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:27.757
state upward, and information only

11
00:00:27.757 --> 00:00:30.439
ever moves up or to the right.

12
00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:34.247
The picture comes from her September 2025 thread,

13
00:00:34.247 --> 00:00:36.998
How information flows through transformers,

14
00:00:36.998 --> 00:00:38.345
written because she found

15
00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:41.098
the usual explainers unhelpful.

16
00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:44.707
Take janus's own example, three words:

17
00:00:44.707 --> 00:00:46.634
You are absolutely.

18
00:00:46.634 --> 00:00:50.187
In GPT-2, each is one token.

19
00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:52.439
Each token gets its own position,

20
00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:55.167
and each position gets its own column.

21
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.767
In her words, positions are "copies of the network

22
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.518
for each token-position in the context."

23
00:01:01.518 --> 00:01:03.271
The copies are identical:

24
00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:05.640
the same network with the same weights.

25
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.834
They differ only in their input token

26
00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:10.017
and where they sit in the text.

27
00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:12.618
At the top, every column outputs

28
00:01:12.618 --> 00:01:14.672
a next-token distribution,

29
00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:17.354
the same kind you saw in part one.

30
00:01:17.354 --> 00:01:19.920
In her diagram, the first predicts are,

31
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.252
the second absolutely, and the third correct.

32
00:01:23.252 --> 00:01:25.447
Every column makes a prediction,

33
00:01:25.447 --> 00:01:27.931
but when the model generates text,

34
00:01:27.931 --> 00:01:31.194
only the last column's prediction is sampled.

35
00:01:31.194 --> 00:01:33.411
The sampled token, correct,

36
00:01:33.411 --> 00:01:36.884
then becomes the input of a fourth column.

37
00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:40.608
Now the rows: each column is a stack of layers,

38
00:01:40.608 --> 00:01:42.222
and the token passes up through

39
00:01:42.222 --> 00:01:44.056
every layer in order.

40
00:01:44.056 --> 00:01:46.227
Every position has every layer,

41
00:01:46.227 --> 00:01:48.247
so the whole picture is a grid:

42
00:01:48.247 --> 00:01:51.661
positions run across, layers run up.

43
00:01:51.661 --> 00:01:53.948
Each layer has its own weights,

44
00:01:53.948 --> 00:01:57.628
and every position in a row uses that same layer.

45
00:01:57.628 --> 00:02:01.111
One cell of the grid is one layer at one position.

46
00:02:01.111 --> 00:02:04.606
Inside each cell, janus draws a purple dot,

47
00:02:04.606 --> 00:02:07.079
a blue box and a fuchsia box;

48
00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:09.726
the next video opens the cell up.

49
00:02:09.726 --> 00:02:11.677
Her drawing shows two layers,

50
00:02:11.677 --> 00:02:13.395
with dots for the rest.

51
00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:15.624
Real models have many more.

52
00:02:15.624 --> 00:02:20.198
GPT-2 small, from 2019, has 12.

53
00:02:20.198 --> 00:02:25.516
The large versions of Llama 3.1 have 80 and 126.

54
00:02:25.516 --> 00:02:27.536
So 80 layers reading a few hundred

55
00:02:27.536 --> 00:02:30.440
tokens make tens of thousands of cells.

56
00:02:30.889 --> 00:02:33.501
What climbs a column is a vector:

57
00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.254
a long list of numbers.

58
00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:37.007
The token enters at the bottom

59
00:02:37.007 --> 00:02:39.039
as one of these vectors.

60
00:02:39.039 --> 00:02:44.217
In GPT-2 small, it is 768 numbers wide.

61
00:02:44.217 --> 00:02:48.722
In the largest Llama 3.1, it is over 16,000.

62
00:02:48.722 --> 00:02:51.613
Each layer reads the vector, computes something,

63
00:02:51.613 --> 00:02:53.831
and adds the result back in.

64
00:02:53.831 --> 00:02:57.198
No layer replaces it; each one only adds.

65
00:02:57.198 --> 00:02:59.984
So the vector at the top is the starting vector

66
00:02:59.984 --> 00:03:03.572
plus everything every layer added along the way.

67
00:03:03.572 --> 00:03:06.393
This running sum is the residual stream.

68
00:03:06.393 --> 00:03:09.063
It is the black arrow in janus's diagram,

69
00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:11.199
and in her words it "flows vertically

70
00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:13.638
through layers at each position."

71
00:03:13.638 --> 00:03:16.436
Its width, called the model's hidden dimension,

72
00:03:16.436 --> 00:03:19.512
limits how much one position can carry at once;

73
00:03:19.512 --> 00:03:21.428
janus calls it the bottleneck.

74
00:03:21.428 --> 00:03:23.378
At the top, the final vector is turned

75
00:03:23.378 --> 00:03:26.085
into the next-token distribution.

76
00:03:26.535 --> 00:03:30.448
Taken alone, a column knows only its own token.

77
00:03:30.448 --> 00:03:32.456
Climbing only its own stream,

78
00:03:32.456 --> 00:03:34.372
the column for absolutely

79
00:03:34.372 --> 00:03:36.798
would never learn that You came first,

80
00:03:36.798 --> 00:03:40.641
yet it needs the earlier words to predict correct.

81
00:03:40.641 --> 00:03:43.671
So something has to carry information sideways,

82
00:03:43.671 --> 00:03:46.307
from earlier columns to later ones.

83
00:03:46.307 --> 00:03:48.884
That is the grid's rule of direction:

84
00:03:48.884 --> 00:03:52.007
inside the network, information moves only up,

85
00:03:52.007 --> 00:03:54.422
through the layers, or to the right,

86
00:03:54.422 --> 00:03:56.234
toward later positions.

87
00:03:56.234 --> 00:04:00.402
Never down: no layer reads from a layer above it.

88
00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:04.175
Never left: no position reads from a later one,

89
00:04:04.175 --> 00:04:05.650
so nothing in the grid sees

90
00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.797
the text that comes after it.

91
00:04:07.797 --> 00:04:10.398
The one route back to the bottom runs outside

92
00:04:10.398 --> 00:04:13.021
the network, through the sampled token,

93
00:04:13.021 --> 00:04:16.377
which the last video in this part returns to.

94
00:04:16.377 --> 00:04:18.339
Up is the residual stream.

95
00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.034
Right is her second highway,

96
00:04:20.034 --> 00:04:23.542
the K/V stream, and that is the next video.

97
00:04:23.992 --> 00:04:26.861
Keep this. One.

98
00:04:27.812 --> 00:04:30.482
A transformer reading text is a grid:

99
00:04:30.482 --> 00:04:32.096
one copy of the same network

100
00:04:32.096 --> 00:04:33.884
at every token position,

101
00:04:33.884 --> 00:04:36.972
each copy a stack of layers. Two.

102
00:04:36.972 --> 00:04:38.504
The residual stream carries

103
00:04:38.504 --> 00:04:41.047
each position's state up its column;

104
00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:43.926
every layer reads it and adds its result.

105
00:04:43.926 --> 00:04:46.132
Three. Inside the network,

106
00:04:46.132 --> 00:04:49.174
information moves only up or to the right:

107
00:04:49.174 --> 00:04:51.555
never down, and never left.
